; 14a-commit.kurt

; `commit` is `break`'s opposite (25-break.kurt): it also closes a
; `sandbox` immediately, without needing a dedent -- but it *keeps* the
; sandbox's content instead of discarding it, via the same selective
; export a `load` uses (16-local.kurt): only a *labelled*, non-`local`
; fact travels to the parent level.

bool C, D

sandbox
    use C "c-holds"     ; labelled -- survives the commit below
    use D               ; unlabelled -- stays scratch, even after commit
    commit

C                        ; fine: "c-holds" was exported

; false            ; would fail: `D` was never exported, so nothing here
;                    talks about it -- same as an unlabelled fact in a
;                    `load`ed file never reaching whoever loads it

; `commit` only ever closes a `sandbox` -- every other block (`proof`,
; `assume`, `let`, `pick`) already has its own way to keep what happened
; inside when it closes (deriving "impl-intro", "forall-intro", ...), so
; there's nothing for `commit` to add there.
